{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>וְאֵלֶּה הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים אֲשֶׁר תָּשִׂים לִפְנֵיהֶם.</b> Why is the section of civil laws juxtaposed to the section about the altar? To tell you that you should place the Sanhedrin next to the altar. Thus Rashi explained, and in tractate Sanhedrin (7a) it is taught: Bar Kappara expounded: From where do we derive that which the Sages said — be deliberate in judgment? For it is written, ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״ (Exodus 20:23), and juxtaposed to it is ״וְאֵלֶּה הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים״. And Rabbi Elazar says: From where do we learn that a judge should not step over the heads of a holy people? For it is written, ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״, and juxtaposed to it is ״וְאֵלֶּה הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים״. And since the Gemara used the term 'expounded' for the words of Bar Kappara, but for the words of Rabbi Elazar it did not use 'expounded,' conclude from this that the words of Bar Kappara are merely a way of derash, for the verse ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״ in its plain sense speaks of the altar — and as for judges, who even mentions their name? But the words of Rabbi Elazar are not derash, and from the plain sense of the verse ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת עַל מִזְבְּחִי״ we learn that its meaning is not to step over the heads of a holy people, as Rashi explained. And indeed this is a matter of a fortiori inference: if concerning stones, which have no awareness to be particular about their own degradation, the Torah said ״לֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״ — do not treat them in a degrading manner — then your fellow, who is in the image of your Creator and is particular about his own degradation, all the more so. And according to this, without any derash at all, we learn that one should not step over the heads of a holy people, by a fortiori inference from the stones." "But in any case Rabbi Elazar also needs the juxtaposition of the sections, for without the juxtaposition I would say that specifically another person who is not a judge and not prominent should not step over the heads of a holy people as a degrading act, by a fortiori inference from the stones — for the commandment ״לֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״ (Exodus 20:23) is addressed to ordinary priests who are not judges, but the judge who needs to instill awe in the public so that his words will be heard — one might have thought that it is permitted for him to step over the heads of a holy people. And perhaps you will say that the rank of the judge is greater than the rank of the priest, and the same reasoning that suffices for the one coming from the law applies to the one being judged — the juxtaposition comes to teach us: ״וְאֵלֶּה הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים אֲשֶׁר תָּשִׂים לִפְנֵיהֶם״ (Exodus 21:1), that is to say: those laws that were mentioned above and are derived by a fortiori inference from the stones — that one should not step over the heads of a holy people — you shall also place לִפְנֵיהֶם, that is, before the judges, to say that the judge too is included in this a fortiori inference. And the view of Bar Kappara is to expound the verse ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״ by way of derash — that the judge must be deliberate in judgment. But his words require explanation, for what does the matter of steps have to do with deliberation? Although Rashi sought to fix this and explained ״בְמַעֲלֹת״ as 'in haste,' it appears that he did not fix it at all, for one who ascends goes slowly, step by step, while one who descends is the one who goes in haste." "And what seems close to me to say regarding this is that the meaning is thus: every judge who does not judge with deliberation does so on account of his arrogance — for he wishes to show everyone that he is expert in the laws and knows how to compare one matter to another, and in the haughtiness of his spirit he will not seek out from a book the substance of the law. And it turns out that this one who does not judge with deliberation is one who ascends the steps by way of pride and arrogance. And this is what Rashi explained as 'in haste' — for he rushes his word to cut the judgment, and his heart is coarse in ruling. And the verse says (Psalms 75:3), ״כִּי אֶקַּח מוֹעֵד אֲנִי מֵישָׁרִים אֶשְׁפֹּט״ — specifically when I take for myself time and an appointed season. And as it is said (Psalms 73:21), ״כִּי יִתְחַמֵּץ לְבָבִי״ — meaning, to make the judgment sour — then ״כִלְיוֹתַי אֶשְׁתּוֹנָן״. And in any case it is difficult for me regarding Rashi, who explained that the juxtaposition of the sections comes to teach that you should place the Sanhedrin next to the altar — from where does Rashi know to say this? Perhaps the juxtaposition comes for the derash of Bar Kappara, or for the words of Rabbi Elazar. And what did Rashi see to make a third explanation and omit those two explanations that are mentioned in the Gemara?" "Therefore it appears to me that Bar Kappara and Rabbi Elazar, both of them hold that what is stated ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת עַל מִזְבְּחִי״ — the word ״עַל״ has two meanings: it can be ״עַל״ literally, which is the plain sense of the verse, and it can mean ״אֵצֶל״ ('next to'), in the manner that ״וְזָבַחְתָּ עָלָיו״ is explained by Rashi as 'next to it,' as in ״וְעָלָיו מַטֵּה מְנַשֶּׁה״ (Numbers 2:20). And one may learn what is below from what is above: just as ״וְעָלָיו״ said above means ״אֵצֶל״, so too ״עַל״ said below also means ״אֵצֶל״. And the juxtaposition of the sections brought them to this explanation, for since it says ״וְאֵלֶּה הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים״ with the conjunctive vav — hear from this that also above some law and ordinance was given to the judges to instruct them in the way they should go, and regarding that it said ״וְאֵלֶּה״ — which is additional to the law and ordinance given to them above — ״תָּשִׂים לִפְנֵיהֶם״, also these ordinances. And if so, a question arises: and where above did He speak of the laws for judges? Rather, it is certain that the place of the Sanhedrin is next to the altar, and above He spoke by way of warning to those who sit next to the altar: ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת עַל מִזְבְּחִי״ — meaning, next to My altar." "And regarding this implication Bar Kappara and Rabbi Elazar disagreed, for the view of Bar Kappara is to explain the verse beyond its plain sense — that it was said to those who sit upon the altar, that is to say, next to the altar: ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״ — do not ascend the steps in haste to judge without deliberation, by way of pride and arrogance, given that you sit upon My altar — next to My altar, which entirely teaches the boundary of humility — both from the fact that it is ״מִּזְבַּח אֲדָמָה״ (Exodus 20:21), and from the fact that ״זִבְחֵי אֱלֹהִים״ (Psalms 51:19) bring about ״רוּחַ נִשְׁבָּרָה״ — and how could it enter your heart to behave with pride and haughtiness in this place, which entirely teaches the boundary of humility. And this explanation is by way of derash, for the plain sense and the derash do not speak of one matter. Therefore Rabbi Elazar came to explain this in such a way that the derash and the plain sense speak of one matter, and his view is to explain that He wished to warn the judge who sits next to the altar: ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״ — do not stride steps by way of coarseness next to the altar, for there is the seat of the Sanhedrin. And one who ascends there on the steps — behold he is stepping over the heads of a holy people, that is to say, over those who are heads over a holy people, namely the Sanhedrin. For according to its plain sense it is difficult: is it only on the head that one should not step, but on the rest of the body it is permitted to step? Rather, ״עַל רָאשֵׁי״ means over those who are heads over a holy people. And without the juxtaposition I would say that in the verse ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״ there is nothing but its plain sense. Also, the verse did not wish to rely on the a fortiori inference that Rashi mentioned — for if so, from where do we learn concerning a judge that he should not step? For from an a fortiori inference one may learn only about any other person, but regarding the judge — perhaps he will err and say: I too am a judge like him, and it is permitted for me to step over him. Therefore the juxtaposition comes to set aside this notion. And it turns out that both according to the words of Bar Kappara and according to the words of Rabbi Elazar, one may learn that the place of the Sanhedrin is next to the altar. Therefore Rashi explained what emerges from the words of both of them." "And there is a further warning concerning the matter of deliberation: that one should not cut the judgment hastily on account of receiving any bribe. For regarding the word שֹׁחַד our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ketubot 105b) that it is derived from the expression שֶׁהוּא חַד, that is, that the giver and the receiver become one, as a single person. But this explanation is not entirely satisfactory, for the word שֹׁחַד refers to the thing that is taken, not to the person who took it. Therefore it appears to me that it is a form of the root ח־ד־ד, sharpening, from the expression ״בַּרְזֶל בְּבַרְזֶל יָחַד״ (Proverbs 27:17) — for every conclusion of judgment is called in the language of the Sages ״חוֹתֵךְ הַדִּין,״ cutting the judgment, and one who is deliberate in judgment and does not cut it hastily is like one who cuts with a knife that is not very sharp, so that he does not cut quickly. But when the judge receives something, immediately his knife becomes sharpened and he cuts the judgment hastily, and he has no need to be deliberate in judgment in order to clarify the truth, for his mind has already agreed to vindicate the one who gave to him. Therefore the money is called ״שׁוֹחַד״ — that it is sharp (חַד), for the money is sharpened and cuts the judgment swiftly. And if you wish to say that the expression ״חַד״ applies to the judge, then its meaning is that from the moment the judge receives from this one, immediately the judge becomes sharpened and sharp-witted and knows at once which way the judgment inclines, and there is no need for the judgment to proceed with delay and deliberation." "Another matter: the reason the money is called שׁוֹחַד — that it is sharp (חַד) — is because our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Avot 5:11): the sword comes upon the world because of the delay of judgment and because of the perversion of judgment. If so, this money is sharp like a sword and cuts like a sharpened sword. And the reason for placing the Sanhedrin adjacent to the altar will be explained in what follows." "<b>Before them.</b> And not before gentiles, even in a matter that the nations judge according to Israelite law, and so forth (Gittin 88b). And this matter requires sufficient explanation. And let us preface the explanation of this matter with what we find in the Midrash (see Esther Rabbah 1:12): that on the throne of King Solomon there were six steps, and on one was written: ״לֹא תַטֶּה מִשְׁפָּט״ (Deuteronomy 16:19); and on the second: ״לֹא תַכִּיר פָּנִים״ (Deuteronomy 16:19); and on the third: ״לֹא תִקַּח שֹׁחַד״ (Deuteronomy 16:19); and on the fourth: ״לֹא תִטַּע לְךָ אֲשֵׁרָה״ (Deuteronomy 16:21); and on the fifth: ״וְלֹא תָקִים לְךָ מַצֵּבָה״ (Deuteronomy 16:22); and on the sixth: ״לֹא תִזְבַּח לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ שׁוֹר וָשֶׂה אֲשֶׁר יִהְיֶה בוֹ מוּם״ (Deuteronomy 17:1). And every discerning person will be astonished at the spectacle: what do these three prohibitions — ״לֹא תִטַּע״ and ״לֹא תָקִים״ and ״לֹא תִזְבַּח״ — have to do with a throne prepared for one who sits in judgment? Is it because they are adjacent in the Torah that they were placed adjacent on the throne? It would have been more fitting had he written in their place ״וְלֹא יַרְבֶּה לּוֹ נָשִׁים״ (Deuteronomy 17:17), ״וְלֹא יַרְבֶּה לּוֹ סוּסִים״ (Deuteronomy 17:16), ״וְכֶסֶף וְזָהָב לֹא יַרְבֶּה לּוֹ״ (Deuteronomy 17:17). From this is a clear proof that all six of these prohibitions speak from the matter of justice. And from where did King Solomon learn to say thus? Surely he understood this from the adjacency of the portion of judgments (מִשְׁפָּטִים) to the portion of the altar (מִזְבֵּחַ) — to teach that you should place the Sanhedrin adjacent to the altar. And what practical consequence emerges from this? That the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded to place the Sanhedrin adjacent to the altar? Surely it is to teach the people this principle: that whoever is disqualified for the altar and for the offerings upon it is also disqualified for the Sanhedrin — and specifically in the matters mentioned at the end of the portion of Yitro concerning the altar." "And with this we may also settle the question: what was the great need to command them concerning the making of the altar? As for the laws of judgment, there was an immediate need; but the altar was not so pressing a need, for they had not yet been commanded concerning the Tabernacle. Rather, surely for this reason the construction of the altar was placed before the portion of judgments — to tell you that you should place the Sanhedrin adjacent to the altar, so that they would learn from the altar that whatever disqualifies for the altar also disqualifies for the Sanhedrin." "The first is this: as it is written ״וְזָבַחְתָּ עָלָיו אֶת עֹלֹתֶיךָ״ and so forth — therefore the offerings were mentioned here, to tell you: just as a blemish disqualifies in the offerings, so a blemish disqualifies also in the Sanhedrin, as is stated in tractate Yevamot (101a): ״Just as the court had to be distinguished in righteousness, so they had to be free of every blemish״ — for thus Solomon said: ״כֻּלָּךְ יָפָה רַעְיָתִי וּמוּם אֵין בָּךְ״ (Song of Songs 4:7). And from where did they learn to say thus? For do we not derive words of Torah from words of the Prophets? Rather, surely King Solomon learned this from the adjacency of this portion and from the adjacency of ״לֹא תִזְבַּח״ (Deuteronomy 17:1) — for in Deuteronomy everyone agrees that we derive from adjacency, even one who does not derive from it elsewhere. And therefore he inscribed upon his throne the verse ״לֹא תִזְבַּח לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ שׁוֹר וָשֶׂה אֲשֶׁר יִהְיֶה בוֹ מוּם״ and so forth (Deuteronomy 17:1), for by this he instructed that all the members of the Sanhedrin who sit upon the throne of judgment had to be free of blemish after the pattern of the offering." "And in the portion of אֱמֹר (Leviticus 21:17) it will be explained, with the help of HaShem, that every physical blemish does not disqualify on its own account, but rather because it points to some bad disposition in the body — whether in an animal or in a person. Therefore it is written ״אֲשֶׁר בּוֹ מוּם כֹּל דָּבָר רָע״, for the blemish points to some bad thing, as stated. And therefore they were careful that the members of the Sanhedrin be free of every blemish. Hence they said: 'Just as they were clean in righteousness, so were they free of blemish.' Why did they link the blemish to righteousness? It must be that the one depends on the other. And by way of allusion we may say that we learn what is obscure from what is explicit: it is written here ״מוּם״, and it is written there (Deuteronomy 15:21) ״וְכִי יִהְיֶה בוֹ מוּם פִּסֵּחַ אוֹ עִוֵּר״ — just as there it speaks of lameness or blindness, so here too. And infer from this as well to a disqualifying blemish in the Sanhedrin, which speaks of one who takes a bribe, for he comes to blindness, and with a crooked scale of justice, for he limps on his thigh and his foot does not stand straight, but rather he hops between the two branches and totters, as it is written (Psalms 15:5) ״וְשֹׁחַד עַל נָקִי לֹא לָקָח, עֹשֵׂה אֵלֶּה לֹא יִמּוֹט לְעוֹלָם״ — by inference, the one who takes a bribe totters, and the nature of a lame person is to totter." "The second is this — that he would write upon his throne ״וְלֹא תָקִים לְךָ מַצֵּבָה״ (Deuteronomy 16:22), that is, one of a single stone — that is to say: do not judge as a solitary judge, for none judges alone except the Holy One, blessed be He; One is He, blessed be His name. And he learned this as well from the juxtaposition of this portion, where it is written ״וְאִם מִזְבַּח אֲבָנִים תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי״ (Exodus 20:22) — implying joined from many stones specifically, and not a monument of a single stone. So too the Sanhedrin — men of excellence who are called ״אַבְנֵי נֵזֶר״, ״אַבְנֵי קֹדֶשׁ״ (Zechariah 9:16) — must be many specifically, and not one. And Rashi explained in the portion of שׁוֹפְטִים, ״אֲשֶׁר שָׂנֵא ה׳״ (Deuteronomy 16:22) — it was beloved in the days of the patriarchs, etc. And a parallel to this regarding judging alone: in the days of the patriarchs the Holy One, blessed be He, was not so strict, for Shem and Eber and Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob judged alone, and so Judah said ״הוֹצִיאוּהָ וְתִשָּׂרֵף״ (Genesis 38:24) — infer from this that he judged alone. And so Moses judged alone before Jethro came, because they were whole in their understanding and possessed the knowledge of their Maker and were not afraid of error. But when hearts diminished and grew ever more deficient, HaShem commanded not to judge alone." "And there is yet another allusion in an altar of stones — that the stones must be whole and not hewn; so too the judge must be whole, free of every blemish, without additions or deficiencies, and not require external embellishment and beautification like a hewn stone, over which the sword has been waved and which is thereby profaned from its sanctity. So too with a judge who requires repair from without: there is room for the axe and the chisel to boast over him, saying, 'I appointed you, and on your own you are not fit for this.' Therefore it is better to choose whole stones that require no repair from without. And Rashi explained that one may derive an a fortiori argument from stones, which do not see, etc., and because they impose peace the Torah said (Deuteronomy 27:5) ״לֹא תָנִיף עֲלֵיהֶן בַּרְזֶל״ — if stones, which impose peace between a man and his wife, between a person and his fellow, are not to be harmed by iron, how much more so should punishment not come upon the judge who imposes peace. It appears that Rashi's intention in this a fortiori argument is to give a reason for the juxtaposition of the Sanhedrin to the altar, for everyone who sits in judgment imposes peace between a person and his fellow. And lest he fear a person — lest the one liable in judgment pursue him, and he wish to remove himself from the case so that harm not befall him — therefore the Sanhedrin sat beside the altar, so that they might learn the a fortiori argument from the altar, that harm should not befall them. And this is a precious interpretation." "The third is ״(וְלֹא תָקִים) [לֹא תִטַּע] לְךָ אֲשֵׁרָה״ — the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted this (Sanhedrin 7a) of one who appoints an unfit judge in the place of Torah scholars, as though he planted an Asherah beside the altar of HaShem. And they also interpreted there the verse ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּן אִתִּי אֱלֹהֵי כֶסֶף״ etc., as referring to a judge who comes for silver and gold. And the matter of the Asherah we learn from what is written ״מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי״ (Exodus 20:21) — either full of earth or adjacent to earth. And from all this we may learn that the Sanhedrin too must be lowly in spirit, for every humble and lowly person lowers his full height to the ground, and therefore it is written (Psalms 25:9) ״יַדְרֵךְ עֲנָוִים בַּמִּשְׁפָּט״. But one whose heart is arrogant in his ruling is straying, wicked, and proud of spirit, for he does not judge with moderation because he is ashamed to ask about his uncertainties. And it is well known that what sometimes causes the appointment of an unfit judge is the haughtiness of his own heart or of his relatives and family who lobby on his behalf, so that he may enjoy prominence in the land, to be for them a name and a praise — as indicated by the name ״אֲשֵׁרָה״ from the root of ״אִשְּׁרוּנִי בָנוֹת״. And the Sages, of blessed memory, said (Sotah 5a) that anyone possessed of arrogance deserves to be cut down like an Asherah: it is written here (Deuteronomy 7:5) ״וַאֲשֵׁרֵיהֶם תְּגַדֵּעוּן״, and it is written there (Isaiah 10:33) ״וְרָמֵי הַקּוֹמָה גְּדוּעִים״. And what has arrogance to do with the Asherah? Rather, because this unfit judge was elevated for the sake of the happiness and praise of his relatives, he is like an Asherah, which has many branches and leaves to sit in its shade. So too they install him as judge so that his relatives may sit in his shade — but in truth their shade has departed from them. And there is no need to elaborate further on this. And as for what they said, ״and in the place of Torah scholars, as though he planted it beside the altar״ — that is because the Sanhedrin sat there. And for this reason they said ״before them and not before gentile courts״ etc., because it is written (Deuteronomy 7:7) ״לֹא מֵרֻבְּכֶם מִכָּל הָעַמִּים חָשַׁק ה׳ בָּכֶם כִּי אַתֶּם הַמְעַט״. And the Sages, of blessed memory, said in tractate Ḥullin (89a) that Israel diminish themselves, but not so the nations, for they are exalted and proud of spirit; therefore they are not fit for laws and judgments, for they do not know them, as their arrogance corrupts all judgments." "And a proof for all this is from a midrash of the Sages, of blessed memory, cited in the Yalkut of the portion of שׁוֹפְטִים (§907), and this is its language: ״שׁוֹפְטִים וְשׁוֹטְרִים תִּתֶּן לְךָ״ — a parable of a king who had many sons, and he loved the youngest more than all of them. And he had one orchard, and he loved it more than all his possessions, and he would give the orchard that he loved to the small son whom he loved. So said the Holy One, blessed be He: Of all the nations I love Israel, as it is written (Hosea 11:1) ״כִּי נַעַר יִשְׂרָאֵל וָאֹהֲבֵהוּ וּמִמִּצְרַיִם קָרָאתִי לִבְנִי״. And it is written (Psalms 37:28) ״כִּי ה׳ אֹהֵב מִשְׁפָּט״ — He gave the laws of justice to Israel. Said the Holy One, blessed be He: If you keep justice, I am exalted, as it is written (Isaiah 5:16) ״וַיִּגְבַּהּ ה׳ צְבָאוֹת בַּמִּשְׁפָּט״ etc. And one must examine this midrash, for it says ״a parable of a king who had many sons״ — if so, he called the nations sons as well, and this matter is not explicit in any verse. And on the contrary, the verse says ״וּמִמִּצְרַיִם קָרָאתִי לִבְנִי״ — infer from this that Israel alone are called sons. Furthermore, from the fact that it says ״and he loved the youngest more than all of them״ — infer from this that he loves the nations too, but not as he loves Israel; and this matter has no proof from Scripture. And on the contrary, the verse says (Malachi 1:2–3) ״וָאֹהַב אֶת יַעֲקֹב וְאֶת עֵשָׂו שָׂנֵאתִי״. And there is a further difficulty: why did he bring as proof the verse ״כִּי נַעַר יִשְׂרָאֵל וָאֹהֲבֵהוּ״? Why did he not bring as proof the verse ״אָהַבְתִּי אֶתְכֶם נְאֻם ה׳״ (Malachi 1:2)?" "And I say that this entire midrash is built on the foundation of humility, to implant it in all who sit in judgment. For its sake it was said 'before them' and not before the nations, for every person of haughty spirit does not judge with deliberation and does not let the judgment ferment to set it quietly on its lees. And this is what was said — a parable of a king who had many children, and the application is not to the nations and Israel, but rather to all Israel, who are called בני אל חי. And of them all, He loves the small one — that is, among all the sons, that is, among all Israel, He loves more than all of them the small one who makes himself small to be humble and lowly of spirit; he is the chosen one to sit in the seat of Elohim in the assembly of judges. Therefore he brings a proof from the verse ״כִּי נַעַר יִשְׂרָאֵל וָאֹהֲבֵהוּ״ (Hosea 11:1) — meaning, when he diminishes himself like a נַעַר, a youth, and a בַּעַר, an ignoramus, then the Holy One, blessed be He, loves him more than all the children of Israel. For as long as humility is more found among the lower ones, then the exaltedness of HaShem, blessed be He, is more revealed and seen, for the humble restore the crown to its rightful place — to the Holy One, blessed be He, who is called עַתִּיק יוֹמִין (Daniel 7:9) — for thereby they acknowledge that He alone, blessed be He, has clothed Himself in majesty, and it befits Him and not another. Therefore he brings as proof the verse ״וַיִּגְבַהּ ה׳ צְבָאוֹת בַּמִּשְׁפָּט״ (Isaiah 5:16), and he says: if you guard the judgment — and this is the language of guarding wine, meaning that your heart will ferment through judging with deliberation, as it is written (Isaiah 56:1) ״שִׁמְרוּ מִשְׁפָּט״, which is likewise the language of שְׁמָרִים, lees — for every drink that stands quietly on its lees, the lees sink below and the drink remains clear; and all vinegar has surely stood a long time on its lees, and for this reason it became vinegar. And of this it is said ״שִׁמְרוּ מִשְׁפָּט״ — for every judgment needs to be fermented and held back until the lees sink below, that is, the dregs and confused opinions intermingled in a person's reason, and once they sink below, his reason will remain clear, pure, and clean without admixture, and then he will not come to error. Therefore it is said ״שִׁמְרוּ מִשְׁפָּט״ — but צְדָקָה, righteous giving, does not ferment: if some poor person asks you for something, do what is asked of you immediately; therefore it is said ״וַעֲשׂוּ צְדָקָה״. And all this will be explained further in parashat Shofetim with the help of HaShem, blessed be He, on the verse ״צֶדֶק צֶדֶק תִּרְדֹּף״ (Deuteronomy 16:20), for the language of pursuit applies to צְדָקָה and not to dinin. And this is what he said: if you guard the judgment, I am exalted — for one who judges with deliberation surely possesses the trait of humility, for he is not ashamed to ask about his uncertainties or to examine the law at length until he truly brings forth the correct ruling. And from the humility of the lower ones the Holy One, blessed be He, is exalted, as it is written (Isaiah 5:15) ״וַיִּשַּׁח אָדָם וַיִּשְׁפַּל אִישׁ״, and immediately after: ״וַיִּגְבַּהּ ה׳ צְבָאוֹת בַּמִּשְׁפָּט״ etc." "And what he said, 'From all the nations I love Israel' — this is not an interpretation of the parable he told of the king who had many children, for after all, 'many children' refers to all Israel; rather, it is an independent matter in its own right. And the mem of מִכָּל is not a superfluous mem. And he brought the nations as proof for his words, saying: just as from among all the nations I did not love any nation at all, but Israel alone I loved, and the root of my hatred for the nations of the world is because of the haughtiness of their heart, for ״תּוֹעֲבַת ה׳ כָּל גְּבַהּ לֵב״ (Proverbs 16:5) — so too, even among Israel, who are called sons, I love more than all of them the small one who diminishes himself and makes himself like a נַעַר, a youth, and a בַּעַר, an ignoramus — for then he judges with deliberation." "And furthermore, a judge who is not haughty of spirit attends to a small matter as to a great one, as it is written (Deuteronomy 1:17) ״כַּקָּטֹן כַּגָּדוֹל תִּשְׁמָעוּן״ — to exclude every person of haughty spirit, who has no need for a small matter. And for this reason the author of this midrash compared judgment to a beloved orchard: for in every orchard, the owner does not single out any one planting as more praiseworthy than its fellow, so that all will engage equally with all the plantings, as is derived in the Yalkut at the beginning of parashat Eikev (Deuteronomy 7:1). And this is precisely the meaning of ״כַּקָּטֹן כַּגָּדוֹל תִּשְׁמָעוּן״, and this is a precious interpretation." "'Before them' — and not before laymen; so it is derived in tractate Gittin (88b). And the implication of this is as though it had already been stated in the verse that it speaks of semukhin, judges of wisdom, and where was this stated? Rather, it surely relies on the passage of the altar that was mentioned before this verse. And we have already said above that we learn from the altar everything stated about it, and whoever is disqualified for the altar is also disqualified for judges. And concerning the altar it says (Exodus 20:22) ״וְאִם מִזְבַּח אֲבָנִים תַּעֲשֶׂה לִי לֹא תִבְנֶה אֶתְהֶן גָּזִית״ — rather, you shall build whole stones, and likewise those who sit by the altar shall be whole stones that require no repair, to exclude laymen who require repair and the like — and perhaps this will help. And just as He disqualified the nations because of the haughtiness of their heart, so too He disqualified the laymen for this reason, for every layman has more arrogance of spirit than an important person, and this way is well known and conceded to the many." "And now, as for the way judges conduct themselves — since it has come to hand to judge, let us say a thing about it. Even though it is not from the subject of the parashah, but from the fact that the verse ״וְאֵלֶּה הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים״ is juxtaposed to the verse ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת״, infer from this that the primary corruption of the judgments depends on arrogance of spirit. And thus our Sages of blessed memory said (Avot 4:9) 'One whose heart is haughty in halachic ruling is a fool, a wicked man, and arrogant of spirit' — meaning that haughtiness of heart will bring him to three corruptions. The first corruption is that his haughtiness will bring him to folly, for he will not wish to judge with deliberation, for it is a disgrace to him, and through this he will mix species with that which is not its species. The second corruption is that his heart will impel him to liken himself to the Most High and to judge alone — and behold, he is wicked, for ״בְּגֹבַהּ אַפּוֹ בַּל יִדְרֹשׁ״ (Psalms 10:4) and ״אֵין אֱלֹהִים כָּל מְזִמּוֹתָיו״ (Psalms 10:4). The third corruption is that he will come to seize the trait of pride, to be arrogant of spirit — not in judgments alone, but in every matter of wisdom he will strut. And we find that the Men of the Great Assembly, when they saw the corruption of the judges of the First Temple — and all this came to them because ״גָבְהוּ בְּנוֹת צִיּוֹן״ (Isaiah 3:16) — therefore they availed themselves of the scepter of the Place, blessed be He. Then they said ״עֵת לַעֲשׂוֹת״ (Psalms 119:126), to restore the crown to its rightful place, that is, to the Holy One, blessed be He, who is called ״עַתִּיק יוֹמִין״ (Daniel 7:9). And therefore they said three things: ״הֱווּ מְתוּנִים בַּדִּין״ (Avot 1:1) — so as not to be a fool reeling in judgment, for perhaps the first product reeling and mixed with lees before it ferments, as explained above (Exodus 20:22). And through this they restored the crown to the Holy One, blessed be He, as we explained above (Exodus 20:22) on the verse ״וַיִּגְבַּהּ ה׳ צְבָאוֹת בַּמִּשְׁפָּט״ (Isaiah 5:16). And they further said ״וְהַעֲמִידוּ תַלְמִידִים הַרְבֵּה״ (Avot 1:1) — so that one need not judge alone, as mentioned. And they further said ״וַעֲשׂוּ סְיָג לַתּוֹרָה״ (Avot 1:1) — so that one not be haughty of spirit at all. For in all traits it is good for a person to follow the middle path, except regarding arrogance of spirit: our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 5a) 'Not a trace of it nor a fraction of it,' and as it is written (Avot 4:4) ״מְאֹד מְאֹד הֱוֵי שְׁפַל רוּחַ״ — to the utmost degree, inclining to the far extreme. And this is the meaning of the fence and the partition, for this trait needs fencing more than all other traits. And therefore they attached this matter to 'one whose heart is haughty in halachic ruling,' for perhaps through this trait he will permit himself to say: it is better for me to conduct myself with arrogance in order to cast my fear upon the litigants, for through this cause my words will be heard. Therefore he said that this is the counsel of the evil inclination, for in the end he will be haughty of spirit in every matter in the world, even outside the judgments." "These three things — in their image and after their likeness — their mention the Psalmist gathered together in the psalm, ״לַמְנַצֵּחַ אַל תַּשְׁחֵת מִזְמוֹר לְאָסָף שִׁיר״ (Psalms 75:1). For corruption of the laws causes the destruction of the world, for the world stands upon the laws, and the destruction of the generation of the Flood proves it. And the Psalmist wanted to recall the rectification of the laws, therefore he said ״אַל תַּשְׁחֵת״ — 'do not destroy.' And he said, ״הוֹדִינוּ לְךָ אֱלֹהִים הוֹדִינוּ״ — the judges, who are called 'אֱלֹהִים,' will give thanks to You for two matters. The first is, ״וְקָרוֹב שְׁמֶךָ״ — that You stand in the congregation of El, in the midst of Elohim He judges. The second is, ״סִפְּרוּ נִפְלְאוֹתֶיךָ״, and this is the language of hiding and wonder — that You hid the laws from every nation and tongue, ״וּמִשְׁפָּטִים בַּל יְדָעוּם״, because the nations in their arrogance do not judge with moderation (מִתּוּן). But as for me, ״כִּי אֶקַּח מוֹעֵד, אֲנִי מֵישָׁרִים אֶשְׁפֹּט״. Therefore he added the word ״אֲנִי״ to exclude the nations and the laymen. ״נְמֹגִים אֶרֶץ וְכָל יֹשְׁבֶיהָ״ — for the one who corrupts the law causes the corruption of the foundation of the world, as it says in that psalm (Psalms 82:2–5), ״עַד מָתַי תִּשְׁפְּטוּ עָוֶל... יִמּוֹטוּ כָּל מוֹסְדֵי אָרֶץ״. Therefore he said there, ״אָנֹכִי תִכַּנְתִּי עַמּוּדֶיהָ סֶּלָה״. And afterward he mentioned 'אַל' three times: ״אָמַרְתִּי לַהוֹלְלִים אַל תָּהֹלּוּ״ — here is one; ״וְלָרְשָׁעִים אַל תָּרִימוּ קָרֶן״ — here are two; ״אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם קַרְנְכֶם״ — here are three. And corresponding to them he said 'כִּי' three times: ״כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא וּמִמַּעֲרָב״ — here is one; ״כִּי אֱלֹהִים שֹׁפֵט״ — here are two; ״כִּי כוֹס בְּיַד ה׳״ — here are three. And since our Sages of blessed memory said, 'One whose heart is proud in halakhic ruling is a fool, an evil person, and arrogant of spirit,' he said here, ״אָמַרְתִּי לַהוֹלְלִים אַל תָּהֹלּוּ״ — to say: do not be a fool who leans on his own wisdom and does not wish to judge with moderation (מִתּוּן), for no person is certain lest there be in his intellect a mixture of dregs (שְׁמָרִים). For לַהוֹלְלִים is an expression of mixture, from the language of ״לִשְׂחוֹק אָמַרְתִּי מְהוֹלָל״ (Ecclesiastes 2:2), which means mixed with sorrow. And here he speaks of being mixed with foolishness; therefore one must let it ferment, lest he drink his wine with its dregs. And since they said that one must not be a wicked person, and that a judge who sits alone resembles the Most High, he said here, ״וְלָרְשָׁעִים אַל תָּרִימוּ קָרֶן״. He mentioned only one קֶרֶן, because he speaks of the wicked person who in his arrogance does not seek the company of his fellows to take counsel in judgment, but wishes to hang everything on himself alone. We may also say that the word ״וְלָרְשָׁעִים״ does not refer to the judges but to the litigants who stand before you as wicked men, and corresponding to them: do not lift up a single horn (קֶרֶן אֶחָד), for perhaps one judge cannot stand against men of might, and two are better than one. And since they said that one whose heart is proud in halakhic ruling will in the end be arrogant of spirit and will stumble in every wisdom, he said here, ״אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם קַרְנְכֶם״ — for the nature of the spirit (רוּחַ) is to rise upward (מָרוֹם); therefore he said, concerning the arrogant of spirit, ״אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם״." "And afterward he gave three reasons for these three, and with what he concluded he opened and said: ״כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא וּמִמַּעֲרָב וְלֹא מִמִּדְבָּר הָרִים״ (Psalms 75:7). And it is concluded in the Yalkut on parashat Mattot (32a) that every instance of 'הָרִים' in Scripture means literal mountains, except for this one, which is an expression of exaltation. So according to our method, he speaks here of the exaltation (רוֹמְמוּת) of the arrogant person, about whom he said, ״אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם קַרְנְכֶם״ (Psalms 75:6). For how can a man pride himself on a vapor of blood, whose beginning is from a putrid drop and whose end is a place of dust, maggots, and worms? And behold, Solomon said concerning the day of death, ״עַד אֲשֶׁר לֹא תֶחְשַׁךְ הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ״ (Ecclesiastes 12:2) — from which it follows that the time of his emergence into the world is the beginning of his sun's shining. Therefore it says, ״כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא״ — that he cannot claim that he has mountains and exaltation from מוֹצָא, that is, from the beginning of his formation, for on the contrary he comes and emerges from a putrid drop. And likewise he has no mountains and exaltation מִמַּעֲרָב, for on the contrary, at the setting (הַעֲרֵב) of his sun he goes to a place of dust, maggots, and worms. And if he should say that between these two boundaries he has room to take pride on account of his Torah and his wisdom — concerning this he said, ״וְלֹא מִמִּדְבָּר הָרִים״: but is there exaltation from the wilderness? After all, everyone tramples upon it, and in tractate Eruvin (54a) our Sages of blessed memory said: 'If a person makes himself like a wilderness, upon which everyone tramples, the Torah is given to him as a gift' — hear from this that for this reason the Torah was given in the wilderness, so that all who learn it would be like this wilderness, upon which everyone tramples. And how can it enter the mind to take mountains and exaltation from the wilderness? Therefore I said, ״אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם קַרְנְכֶם״." "And afterward he said, corresponding to what is written, ״וְלָרְשָׁעִים אַל תָּרִימוּ קָרֶן״ (Psalms 75:5) — 'יחידי' — that one must not judge alone. Concerning this he gave a reason and said, ״כִּי אֱלֹהִים שֹׁפֵט״ (Psalms 75:8) — meaning that there is no single judge except One, blessed be He, and in His hand is to bring low and to lift up, and who can say to Him, 'What do You do?'" "And afterward he said, corresponding to what is written, ״אָמַרְתִּי לַהוֹלְלִים אַל תָּהֹלּוּ״ (Psalms 75:5) — that one must not judge before letting the judgment ferment, while his intellect is still mixed with the dregs. Concerning this he said, ״כִּי כוֹס בְּיַד ה׳ וְיַיִן חָמַר מָלֵא מֶסֶךְ וַיַּגֵּר מִזֶּה״ (Psalms 75:9) — for the cup of the Holy One, blessed be He, is clean of all dregs, and ״וַיַּגֵּר מִזֶּה״ — to pour out His spirit upon the flesh of a spirit (רוּחַ) that is pure and clean of all mixtures. ״אַךְ שְׁמָרֶיהָ יִמְצוּ יִשְׁתּוּ כֹּל רִשְׁעֵי אָרֶץ״ (Psalms 75:9) — these are the הוֹלְלִים, whose wine of intellect is mixed with dregs, and the wicked men who do not judge with moderation (מִתּוּן) to let the judgment ferment — it is as though they drank the wine while it is still mixed with the dregs, and in the confusion and clouding of the intellect, they cause the distortion of the judgment.<br>״וַאֲנִי אַגִּיד לְעוֹלָם״ (Psalms 75:10) — for above he said of himself, ״אֲנִי מֵישָׁרִים אֶשְׁפֹּט״ (Psalms 75:3); he gave yet another straight path before a man who sits in judgment, and said, ״אֲנִי אַגִּיד לְעוֹלָם״ — to speak hard things, like sinews (כְּגִידִין), to the world (לְעוֹלָם) with both litigants, for when they stand before me they are as wicked men in my eyes, and I will not fear the powerful litigant while a wicked man stands opposite me; ״כִּי אֲזַמְּרָה לֵאלֹהֵי יַעֲקֹב״ (Psalms 75:10) — who delivered Jacob from the hand of one stronger than himself and from his pursuers, He will deliver me as well from the pursuer. Nevertheless, let not the wicked man rely upon this to say: since I speak harshly to his fellow too, perhaps his fellow also appears guilty in my eyes — rather, ״וְכָל קַרְנֵי רְשָׁעִים אֲגַדֵּעַ תְּרוֹמַמְנָה קַרְנוֹת צַדִּיק״ (Psalms 75:11) — for in the end I declare the wicked man guilty and I declare the righteous man innocent. And everything alluded to in this psalm is alluded to in these short words that our Sages of blessed memory expounded: ״לִפְנֵיהֶם״ — and not before gentiles; ״לִפְנֵיהֶם״ — and not before laymen. And the discerning one will hear and add lesson."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {9 "Resolved the Talmudic abbreviation ׳(קא)׳ as Yevamot 101a based on the well-known passage there about the physical requirements of judges.", 13 "In the opening lemma the text appears to contain a scribal cross-reference: the bracketed לֹא תִטַּע is the correct prohibition of Asherah (Deuteronomy 16:21) while וְלֹא תָקִים refers to the prior verse about a matzevah; both have been rendered as found.", 20 "The opening phrase 'these three things' refers to the three derashot our Sages derive from the repetition of 'לִפְנֵיהֶם' in Exodus 21:1 — before them and not before gentiles, before them and not before laymen, before them and not before a lone judge — which the Kli Yakar proceeds to map onto Psalm 75."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:56.110829434Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}